NNAAMMEE
     ffssiirraanndd — randomize inode generation numbers

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ffssiirraanndd [--bbffpp] _s_p_e_c_i_a_l _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffssiirraanndd command installs random generation numbers on all the inodes
     for each filesystem specified on the command line by _s_p_e_c_i_a_l.  This in‐
     creases the security of NFS-exported filesystems by making it difficult
     to “guess” filehandles.

     _N_o_t_e: newfs(8) now does the equivalent of ffssiirraanndd itself so it is no
     longer necessary to run ffssiirraanndd by hand on a new filesystem.  It is only
     used to re-randomize or report on an existing filesystem.

     ffssiirraanndd should only be used on an unmounted filesystem that has been
     checked with fsck(8) or a filesystem that is mounted read-only.  ffssiirraanndd
     may be used on the root filesystem in single-user mode but the system
     should be rebooted via “reboot -n” afterwards.

     The options are as follows:

     --bb      Use the default block size (usually 512 bytes) instead of the
             value gleaned from the disklabel.

     --ff      Force ffssiirraanndd to run even if the filesystem on _s_p_e_c_i_a_l is not
             marked as clean.

     --pp      Print the current generation numbers for all inodes instead of
             generating new ones.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     fs(5), fsck(8), newfs(8), reboot(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ffssiirraanndd command appeared in SunOS 3.x.  This version of ffssiirraanndd first
     appeared in OpenBSD 2.1.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     Todd C. Miller

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
     Since ffssiirraanndd allocates enough memory to hold all the inodes in a given
     cylinder group, it may use a large amount of memory for large disks with
     few cylinder groups.

